  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "One English word covers coins and notes in hand, money in a bank, and wealth in general. Some languages separate cash from money in general, or use the name of a currency unit as the general word. English cash exists for coins and notes only.",
    "grammar": "Uncountable: much money, a little money, never a money or many money. A singular verb: the money was gone. Amounts are counted in units (three dollars) or as sums (a large sum of money).",
    "culture": "How much money a person earns or has is treated as private in most English-speaking countries; asking a new acquaintance about their salary is rude. Money before a noun (money problems, money worries) usually means a shortage of it.",
    "false_friends": "In some languages a look-alike word means a coin or a country's currency rather than money in general.",
    "pronunciation": "The o is pronounced as the u of sun, not the o of go: MUN-ee. Stress on the first syllable; the ending sounds like a short ee."
  },
